  <!-- ══════════ 4 · PLAN ══════════ -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="sec-num">04</div>
      <h2>The plan, in order</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <div class="rail">Execution</div>
      <div class="flow">
        <p>
          Two stop conditions: Phase 3 can't start before Phase 1 lands (the invariants can't hold
          while RC-1 is live), and Phase 4 can't start before Phase 2 (the grid needs the tokens).
          Phases 5 and 6 are independent of each other.
        </p>

        <div class="phases">

          <div class="phase first">
            <div class="phase-n">0</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Land the gate first — failing</h3>
              <p>
                Write <code>scripts/verify-mobile-responsive.mjs</code> before touching any UI, and
                record today's numbers as the baseline. A fix with no gate is a fix with a
                half-life.
              </p>
              <p class="gate"><b>Gate:</b> probe runs and reports the current failures</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="phase first">
            <div class="phase-n">1</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Remove RC-1 without losing touch accessibility</h3>
              <p>
                The 44&nbsp;px rule exists for a real reason. Give the hit area back without touching
                the layout box — a pseudo-element is part of its element for hit-testing, so the tap
                region grows while the painted box and every sibling's position stay as designed.
                Then audit the rest of the 640&nbsp;px block as one unit; keep the
                <code>font-size: 16px</code> on inputs that prevents the iOS focus zoom.
              </p>
              <pre><code>.tap-target { position: relative; }
.tap-target::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  width: max(100%, 44px); height: max(100%, 44px);
}</code></pre>
              <p class="gate"><b>Gate:</b> the new probe + a full visual sweep — this changes every mobile page</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="phase">
            <div class="phase-n">2</div>
            <div>
              <h3>The responsive foundation</h3>
              <p>
                One token layer on <code>.tp-viewport</code>: a design ratio
                <code>--u: clamp(0.84, calc(100cqi / 390), 1.10)</code> driving spacing and type, the
                <code>svh</code>/<code>dvh</code> fallback chain, and real safe-area insets.
                Container units rather than viewport units, so the same components stay correct
                inside the landing page's phone demo and on a tablet.
              </p>
              <p class="gate"><b>Gate:</b> no horizontal scroll, no edge-crossing box, no clipped text — six sizes</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="phase">
            <div class="phase-n">3</div>
            <div>
              <h3>The action bar, unable to diverge</h3>
              <p>
                One shared <code>SegmentedBar</code> for all three verticals. Put the buttons and the
                indicator in the same CSS grid row so the bubble's height <em>is</em> the button's
                height — whatever RC-1-shaped rule appears next. Keep measurement only for the
                sliding transform, and add a <code>ResizeObserver</code> plus a
                <code>document.fonts.ready</code> re-measure. The split-bill pill takes
                <code>var(--bar-h)</code>, so "exactly the height of the action bar" becomes a token,
                not two numbers that happen to match.
              </p>
              <p class="gate"><b>Gate:</b> bar height fixed; bubble ≡ active button; label fully inside bubble; send ≡ bar; never clipped; all still true after a resize</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="phase">
            <div class="phase-n">4</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Terminal home: measured gutters, three-row floor</h3>
              <p>
                Replace <code>height: 50%</code> + magic padding with a three-row grid whose middle
                row is the <em>measured</em> height of whatever chrome is actually floating, and
                whose stack row has
                <code>min-height: calc(3 × var(--row-h) + var(--stack-hdr-h))</code>. "At least
                three" stops being an outcome and becomes a CSS invariant. Pin the header outside the
                scroll container. On short screens the hero contracts first — the stack floor is
                non-negotiable.
              </p>
              <p class="gate"><b>Gate:</b> <code>visibleStackRows &gt;= 3</code> on all six sizes</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="phase">
            <div class="phase-n">5</div>
            <div>
              <h3>The money display fits its box</h3>
              <p>
                <code>white-space: nowrap</code> plus
                <code>font-size: min(var(--amount-max), calc(100cqi / var(--amount-chars) × k))</code>,
                with React setting the character count. Deterministic, no measurement, no reflow
                loop, trivially assertable. A <code>useFitText</code> fallback covers late font loads,
                with a floor below which the presentation compacts rather than shrinking into
                illegibility. Applies to all ten call sites, not just the hero.
              </p>
              <p class="gate"><b>Gate:</b> the amount's box sits inside its parent at the maximum value, six sizes</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="phase">
            <div class="phase-n">6</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Scope the stylesheets, extract the primitives</h3>
              <p>
                Scope retail and property under their own class the way trades already is; better,
                move all three out of template literals into real <code>.css</code> files so Vite
                dedupes them and they stop being re-parsed on every mount. Then extract the shared
                primitives — bar, amount, stack row, pill, field, CTA — so the next fix lands once
                instead of three times.
              </p>
              <p class="gate"><b>Gate:</b> no unscoped <code>.tp-</code> selector survives the grep</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="phase">
            <div class="phase-n">7</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Make the gate permanent</h3>
              <p>
                Six viewports × every mobile route × both orientations, with the component contracts
                as hard assertions, a re-measure after a size change, and runs with a simulated safe
                area and a collapsed dock. Plus four cheap CI greps that catch the <em>cause</em>:
                no <code>min-height</code> on a bare element selector inside a media query; no new
                <code>fontSize</code> literals or <code>height: '50%'</code> under
                <code>features/terminal/</code>; no <code>100vh</code> on a phone surface; no
                unscoped <code>.tp-</code> rule in a style literal.
              </p>
              <p class="gate"><b>Gate:</b> wired in beside <code>verify-desktop-p0.mjs</code>, exiting non-zero</p>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ 5 · DECISIONS ══════════ -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="sec-num">05</div>
      <h2>Four calls that are yours</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <div class="rail">Decisions</div>
      <div class="flow">
        <p>
          These change the shape of the work. The plan above assumes the recommended answer, and a
          different answer only changes Phases 2, 4 and 5.
        </p>

        <div class="decision">
          <h3>D1 · Adaptation strategy</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Fluid / intrinsic layout</strong><span class="rec">recommended</span> — tokens,
            <code>clamp()</code>, container queries, measured gutters. Everything reflows and type
            stays a readable physical size on every device.
          </p>
          <p>
            The alternative is the scaled canvas the desktop app already uses: design once at 390×844
            and <code>transform: scale()</code> to fit. Cheap and pixel-perfect — but text goes
            physically tiny on a 320&nbsp;px phone and tap targets scale below the minimum. Right for
            simulating a laptop on a tablet; wrong for a real phone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="decision">
          <h3>D2 · Smallest supported phone</h3>
          <p>
            The plan assumes <strong>320×568</strong> — iPhone SE 1st gen, older Android, and any
            iPhone in Display Zoom. That's the size where three stack rows plus a hero genuinely
            don't fit without the hero contracting, and it may need a compact row variant. If
            360×640 is the real floor, every clamp minimum relaxes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="decision">
          <h3>D3 · Blast radius of the RC-1 fix</h3>
          <p>
            Removing the blanket rule changes the rendered height of 5–13 buttons on
            <em>every</em> mobile page — mostly back toward the original design. One sweeping commit
            plus the gate<span class="rec">recommended</span>, because a half-applied fix leaves two
            inconsistent button systems live at the same time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="decision">
          <h3>D4 · Money formatting</h3>
          <p>
            <code>fmt()</code> produces <code>$99999.99</code> with no thousands separator. Should it
            be <code>$99,999.99</code>? Grouping widens the string, so the fitter has to be
            calibrated for whichever you want. Current maximum is 7 digits.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

